### Context / why they ask this
C++ is the language of production pricing libraries. Interviewers for quant developer and strat roles ask design questions to see whether you can structure code, not only write loops. The classic reference is Joshi's "C++ Design Patterns and Derivatives Pricing", built exactly around this example.

### Key concepts
- **Abstract base class**: has at least one pure virtual function (`= 0`); cannot be instantiated.
- **Virtual function / dynamic dispatch**: the call is resolved at run time through the object's vtable.
- **Virtual destructor**: needed so deleting a derived object through a base pointer runs the derived destructor.
- **Virtual copy constructor (`clone`)**: copying through a base pointer without knowing the concrete type.
- **RAII** (resource acquisition is initialisation): resources are released in destructors; smart pointers apply it to memory.
- **Rule of zero / three / five**: if a class manages a resource, define (or delete) destructor, copy and move operations; better, hold resources in members that manage themselves, and define none.
- **Const-correctness**: mark methods and references `const` when they do not modify state.
- **Templates / static polymorphism**: type resolved at compile time; CRTP (curiously recurring template pattern) is one idiom.

### The design (sketch)
```cpp
class Payoff {
public:
    virtual ~Payoff() = default;
    virtual double operator()(double spot) const = 0;
    virtual std::unique_ptr<Payoff> clone() const = 0;
};

class CallPayoff : public Payoff {
public:
    explicit CallPayoff(double k) : k_(k) {}
    double operator()(double s) const override { return std::max(s - k_, 0.0); }
    std::unique_ptr<Payoff> clone() const override {
        return std::make_unique<CallPayoff>(*this);
    }
private:
    double k_;
};

class VanillaOption {
public:
    VanillaOption(const Payoff& p, double expiry) : payoff_(p.clone()), expiry_(expiry) {}
    VanillaOption(const VanillaOption& o) : payoff_(o.payoff_->clone()), expiry_(o.expiry_) {}
    double payoff(double s) const { return (*payoff_)(s); }
    double expiry() const { return expiry_; }
private:
    std::unique_ptr<Payoff> payoff_;
    double expiry_;
};

double mcPrice(const VanillaOption& opt, double s0, double r, double vol,
               std::size_t nPaths, std::mt19937_64& rng);
```
The engine draws normals, builds $S_T=S_0e^{(r-\sigma^2/2)T+\sigma\sqrt TZ}$, calls `opt.payoff(ST)` and accumulates mean and variance (a separate statistics class lets you add confidence intervals or convergence tables without touching the engine). Parameters such as vol can be wrapped in a `Parameters` class to allow time-dependent vol later.

### Design choices and trade-offs
- **Why `clone()`**: `VanillaOption` must own a copy of an arbitrary payoff; copying a `Payoff&` would slice it to the base class.
- **Copy semantics with a `unique_ptr` member**: `unique_ptr` is move-only, so the implicit copy assignment of `VanillaOption` is deleted, and declaring the copy constructor also suppresses the implicit move operations (moves silently fall back to copies). Either add `operator=` (copy-and-swap with `clone()`) plus `= default` moves (rule of five), or hide the cloning inside a small `clone_ptr<T>` value wrapper and write none of them (rule of zero).
- **Why `unique_ptr` rather than `shared_ptr`**: single ownership is clearer and cheaper; `shared_ptr` is for genuinely shared, immutable objects (e.g. a market data curve used by many trades).
- **Virtual vs template**: an indirect call costs a few nanoseconds; generating one normal costs tens. So virtual payoffs are fine at the path level. In hot inner loops (per time step, per path of millions), templates allow inlining and SIMD. Trade-offs: templates need the type at compile time, increase compile time and binary size, and produce harder error messages.
- **Randomness**: pass the generator explicitly (reproducibility, thread safety); one generator per thread with independent streams.
- **Testing**: put-call parity and Black-Scholes closed forms as unit tests; convergence as $1/\sqrt n$.

### Common pitfalls
- Missing virtual destructor: undefined behaviour when deleting through a base pointer.
- Raw `new`/`delete` with manual copy constructors: leaks and double frees; use smart pointers.
- Passing large objects by value; pass `const&` instead.
- Object slicing when storing derived objects by value in a `std::vector<Payoff>`: store `unique_ptr<Payoff>`.

### How to deliver it
Start from the principle ("payoff, model and engine are independent"), draw the base class with its three members (virtual destructor, `operator()`, `clone`), then ownership, then the virtual vs template trade-off with the "a random number costs more than a virtual call" argument. Offer to write the Monte Carlo loop if they want code.

